  "theory": {
        "intro": "To differentiate a sum or difference, differentiate each term separately. A constant multiple stays out the front: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}(kf(x)) = kf'(x)$. Expand brackets or split fractions first, so every term is a power of $x$.",
        "facts": [
          "**Sum rule**: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(f(x) + g(x)\\right) = f'(x) + g'(x)$.",
          "**Difference rule**: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(f(x) - g(x)\\right) = f'(x) - g'(x)$.",
          "**Constant multiple rule**: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(k \\cdot f(x)\\right) = k \\cdot f'(x)$ for any constant $k$.",
          "These combine: $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(af(x) + bg(x)\\right) = af'(x) + bg'(x)$.",
          "Differentiate a polynomial term by term, in any order of powers.",
          "Expand brackets first, so a product becomes a sum of power terms.",
          "Divide each term of the numerator by a single-term denominator before differentiating.",
          "A constant differentiates to $0$, while a term $cx$ differentiates to $c$."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Expand brackets or split single-denominator fractions so each term is in the form $kx^n$.",
          "Differentiate each term independently using the power rule.",
          "Simplify coefficients and combine like terms if applicable."
        ]
      },
